<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chechnya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chechen Republic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chechnya is a republic of the Russian Federation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a>, with its capital at Grozny. Its people fought Russian conquest in the 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasian-war">Caucasian War</a>, many under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Imam Shamil</a>, were deported en masse to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> in 1944, and fought two wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> after declaring independence in 1991. Since 2007 it has been ruled by Ramzan Kadyrov.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chechnya.jpg" alt="Chechnya" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Danloud · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_map_of_Chechnya,_Russia_(2014%E2%80%932022).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chechnya lies in the eastern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a>. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> to the east, Ingushetia and North Ossetia-Alania to the west, Stavropol Krai to the northwest and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> to the south. It is divided between flatter country north of the Terek river and highlands to the south, and the Terek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunzha">Sunzha</a> and Argun are its main rivers. Traces of human settlement dating back to 40,000 BCE have been found near Lake Kezenoyam, and cave paintings and artefacts indicate continuous habitation for some 8,000 years. Georgian tradition, in the chronicle of Leonti Mroveli, names a forefather of the Vainakhs, the ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a> and Ingush, as Kavkas, from whom the word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> was said to derive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 14th and 15th centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a> fought repeatedly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Tamerlane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>. In the mid-14th century a Chechen princedom called Simsim arose under Khour II, who defeated the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> at the Battle of Tatar-tup in 1362. Khour II later allied himself with Tokhtamysh, and Timur invaded Simsim in 1395 to punish the highlanders. Chechen tribes built fortresses, castles and defensive walls in the mountains. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> sought to extend Russian influence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> in the Russo-Persian War of 1722–1723.</p><p>Under the Soviet Union Chechnya and Ingushetia were joined as the Checheno-Ingush Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic. In 1944, near the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a> and Ingush were accused of supporting Nazi Germany, an accusation described as false, and whole nations were deported to the Kazakh SSR in Operation Lentil; over 60 percent of the Chechen and Ingush populations are said to have died.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a> the republic split into Ingushetia and the Chechen Republic, which proclaimed itself the independent Chechen Republic of Ichkeria. In the First Chechen War of 1994–1996 Russian forces failed to establish permanent control of the mountains. Its president, Dzhokhar Dudayev, was killed in April 1996, and after Aslan Maskhadov's forces retook Grozny, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-yeltsin">Boris Yeltsin</a> declared a ceasefire and signed a peace treaty a year later. Federal control was restored in the Second Chechen War of 1999–2009. A constitution that kept Chechnya within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> with limited autonomy was put to a referendum on 23 March 2003.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chechnya is a republic of the Russian Federation with its capital at Grozny, and about 1.5 million inhabitants, nearly all of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a>. The languages in use are Chechen, which belongs to the Vainakh branch of the Caucasian languages together with Ingush, and Russian. Most of the people are Sunni Muslims who follow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi schools</a> of jurisprudence, and many belong to the Qadiri or Naqshbandi Sufi orders.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chechnya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shamil built a centralised theocracy across mountainous Dagestan and <b>Chechnya</b>, dividing it into districts under naibs, levying taxes, raising a standing army and replacing customary law with sharia.</blockquote><a href="/state/caucasian-imamate">Caucasian Imamate</a></li><li><blockquote>After Denikin's Volunteer Army overran the Mountainous Republic of the Northern Caucasus in 1919, the elderly Naqshbandi sheikh Uzun-Haji, who had long called for a revival of Shamil's imamate, proclaimed the North Caucasian Emirate in September 1919 in the mountains of <b>Chechnya</b>, with its seat at Vedeno.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-caucasian-emirate">North Caucasian Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
